-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the normalised level transport
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up over a large body of ambient data, which is described group by group; the conclusion is stated in full at the end.
--
--   **Base arithmetic data.** Two distinct primes $r$, $\bar r$ and a nonzero natural number $N$, with $\bar r \neq r$, $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ of $\mathcal O$, completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $\pi$-adic filtration (`hcomplete`), residue cardinality $\#(\mathcal O/\pi) = r$ (`hres`) and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. A characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). A monoid homomorphism $vdet : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) which, by `hvdet`, computes the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant: $vdet\,g = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Indefinite quaternion algebra and moduli data.** Rationals $a,b$ with `hB`: $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies $0 < a$ or $0 < b$ and, at a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$, is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$. A maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`: an order which is maximal among orders containing it). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point assignment $pt$ on fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level $N$ over arbitrary commutative rings, such that `h𝒳` holds: $(\mathcal X, f, pt)$ is a coarse moduli datum (isomorphism-invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). A level $n$ with $3 \le n$, $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point assignment $ptF$ on pairs (curve, full level-$n$ structure) which is a fine moduli datum (`hM`); a group $G$ acting by automorphisms $\rho$ of $M$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying `hρ`, the level-twist axioms (the action is over the base, it realises twisting of full level structures by $\chi(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$). A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over the base (`hp`), invariant under the $G$-action (`hρp`) and compatible with the two point assignments (`hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ a coarse moduli datum $(\mathcal Y_\ell, g_\ell, ptT_\ell)$ for curves with extra level $\ell$ (`h𝒴`), together with two degeneracy morphisms $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on points forget the extra level (`hd₀`) and divide by it along a level isogeny (`hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $ar$, $a\bar r$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realising on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite quaternion algebra and group-theoretic data.** Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is a division algebra at a finite place exactly when that place lies over $\bar r$. A maximal order $\Lambda_1$ and an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$; an idelic unit $n_1$ in $primeHeckeSet\,R_1\,r$ with $meetOrder\,R_1\,n_1 = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ Eichler of level $N r$ (`hS₁`). An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$, and a height-one prime $v$ of the integers of $\mathbb Q$ containing $r$ (`hv`). A subgroup $\Gamma t$ characterised by `hΓt` as $awayUnits\,R_1\,v$, the intersection over all finite places $w \neq v$ of the preimages of the subgroups generated by the local unit boxes of $R_1$ at $w$. Families $s$ and $sf$ indexed by the primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, subject to `hs` (four clauses): the local component of $sf_\ell$ at each place not over $r$ is $s_\ell$, its component at the places over $r$ is $1$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in $levelHeckeUSet\,\Lambda_1\,(meetOrder\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ if $\ell \mid N$ and in $primeHeckeSet\,(meetOrder\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$. The groups $\Gamma t_\ell = \Gamma t \cap s_\ell \Gamma t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). A unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma t$ (`hwbar`). The hypothesis `hΛℤ` that all rational integers lie in $\Lambda$, and a map $coord : \Lambda \to \mathbb Z_{r^2} \times \mathbb Z_{r^2}$ (values in Witt vectors over the field with $r^2$ elements) satisfying `hcoord`, i.e. $coord$ is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, satisfies the twisted multiplication rule involving $r$ and the Witt Frobenius, is injective, has dense image modulo all powers of $r$, and is compatible with reduced traces.
--
--   **Reduction data at $r$.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $Onr/\pi$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ over $Onr/\pi$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, with `hA₀`: $\theta_0$ presents the formal completion of $A_0$ as $X_0$, compatibly with $coord$ on the $\Lambda$-action. Families $e, e' : \Gamma t \to \mathrm{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`he`) and degrees $deg$, subject to: `hE1` (each $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{deg\,\gamma}$ and $e\gamma$ preserves the level structure); `hE1mul` (multiplicativity of $e$ up to composition with multiplication by powers of $r$); `hE1sc` (for $\gamma$ a rational integer scalar $c$, $e\gamma$ agrees with multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$); `hE1'` (conversely, if $e\gamma$ is a scalar up to powers of $r$ then $\gamma$ is a scalar in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$); `hE2` (for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $X_0$-action and $\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$); `hE3` (every full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ admits a labelling $lab : \Gamma t \to \Lambda$ with $e\gamma$ acting on $P_0$ as $lab\,\gamma$, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$); `hE4` (over every algebraically closed field over $Onr/\pi$, every level-preserving isogeny pair of $r$-power degree on a curve pulled back from $A_0$ comes from some $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$). Further, an order $R_2 \le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) such that every element of $R_1$ enters $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), with endomorphisms $\hat e : R_2 \to \mathrm{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`hê`), subject to `hE5a` (additivity on points, commutation with the $\Lambda$-action, preservation of level), `hE5b1`–`hE5b3` ($\hat e(1) = \mathrm{id}$, $\hat e(xy) = \hat e(y)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$, and $\hat e$ of a rational integer is multiplication by that integer), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility of $\hat e$ with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\Gamma t$) and `hE5e` (a joint version of `hE2` providing a single $g$ matching both the $e\gamma$ and the $\hat e(x)$ through $E_0$, again up to powers of $r$).
--
--   **Drinfeld-side moduli package.** A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to Onr$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the induced structure map (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), together with $\eta$ assigning to rigidified objects $t$ over $r$-nilpotent bases points of $MD$, subject to `hη` (three clauses: on admissible objects $\eta$ identifies exactly the isomorphic ones; $\eta$ is compatible with base change; and every point of $MD$ is, after passing to a Zariski cover given by finitely many elements generating the unit ideal, of the form $\eta\,t$ for an admissible $t$). An injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and $\Phi.varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the precise sense of `hE₀` (a single exponent $m$ with $r^{m}M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the image and $r^{m}$ times the image contained in $M_2(\mathcal O)$). A family $eD$ of maps from the functor $ModuliPackage.G\;\mathcal O\;MD$ to the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ with the Deligne-datum functor $Omega\,K_0\,\pi$, natural in $\mathcal O$-algebra maps (`hnatD`), bijective on every Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra (`hbijD`), with first component the structure map $\psi$ (`hfstD`), turning the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action $ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy$ into the twisted action $OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct$ (`hGLD`), turning $\pi$-translation into the Frobenius twist of the first component with the Deligne datum unchanged (`hPiD`), and such that $\pi$-translates always exist (`hPiexD`). A ring homomorphism $\kappa : Onr/\pi \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to X_0$ pushed along $\kappa$, of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$ in the reduction.** A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ over $Onr/\pi$ with morphisms $aw$, $aw'$ over the base exhibiting $A_0 \to A_{0w}$ as the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), a natural number $kw$ and morphisms $bw$, $bw'$ forming an isogeny pair of degree $r^{kw}$ from $A_{0w}$ to $A_0$ (`hBSw`) with $bw$ preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ being over the base (`habw`), and `hJOINTw`, a joint version of `hE2` in which a single $g$ simultaneously matches all $e\gamma$ with $g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$ and the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ with $g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g^{-1}$, in each case up to a power of $r$.
--
--   **The comparison map $\Xi$.** A map $\Xi$ sending, for each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each $\psi : Onr \to B$, a rigidified curve over $(B,\psi)$ to a point of $ModuliPackage.GPoint\;\mathcal O\;MD$ over $B$, subject to: `hΞleg` (over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents, the structure map of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $Fr^{k}$ of $\psi$ for some $k \in \mathbb Z$); `hΞnat` (compatibility with pullback of rigidified curves along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps); `hΞiso` ($\Xi$ takes the same value on two rigidified curves over $(B,\psi)$ whose underlying curves are isomorphic in the level- and $\Lambda$-compatible sense and whose rigidifications are related by the displayed comparison diagram up to powers of $r$); `hΞdef` (over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents and $r$ nilpotent, if the rigidified curve carries a formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ through $coord$, then there are $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ over $B$ with $t.X = X$, with $\theta$ a rigidity transport of $\theta_0$ along $\kappa$, $\beta_0$ at parity $j$, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the twist $Fr^{-j}\psi$, and with $\Xi$ of the curve equal to the point built from $Fr^{-j}\psi$ and $\eta\,t$). An element $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, together with `heqΓ` (over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents, if two rigidifications of the same curve are translates of one another by $e\gamma$, then their $\Xi$-images are related by the $ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy$ action of $r^{c} \cdot g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g_0^{-1}$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$) and `heqW` (likewise, if $\bar r$ is invertible in $B$ and $E \to E_f$ is an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ whose rigidifications are related by the displayed diagram involving $aw$ followed by $bw$ up to powers of $r$, then the $\Xi$-images are related by the action of $r^{c} \cdot g_0\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g_0^{-1}$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every commutative Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, every $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : Onr \to B$, every fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$ with $\Lambda$-action and level $N$, every rigidification $\varrho$ of $E$ relative to $A_0$ along the twisted structure map $frobTwist\;Onr\;Fr\;(-vdet(g_0))\;\psi$, and all full level-$n$ structures $P_n$, $P_n'$ on $E$: if $P_n$ and $P_n'$ both satisfy $FakeEllipticCurve.Rigidification.IsNormLevelTransport\;Fr\;\theta_0\;\kappa\;\beta_0\;\Phi\;\iota\;coord\;P_0\;\varrho$, then the underlying sections agree: $P_n.P = P_n'.P$.
--
--   Here the transport condition for a full level-$n$ structure $P$ says: there is a section $Q$ of $\varrho.Ab$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(B/\pi B)$ with $Q$ followed by $\varrho.gA$ equal to the base change of $P_0.P$ along the residue map $Onr/\pi \to B/\pi B$ induced by $\psi$, and there exist a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ over $B$, formal coordinates $\theta$ of dimension $2$ on $E$ presenting its completion as $X$ through $coord$, an integer $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ over $B$ with $t.X = X$, with $(\varrho,\theta,j,t)$ a rigidity transport of $\theta_0$ along $\kappa$ and $\beta_0$, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the twist $Fr^{-j}\psi$, and such that the reduction modulo $\pi$ of $r^{t.n}\cdot P$ equals $Q$ followed by $\varrho.\varphi'$ and $\varrho.gb$. The remaining fields of $FullLevel$ are propositions, so the asserted equality of sections pins down the level structure itself.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the construction of the height-normalised level transport in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison: the condition that a full level-$n$ structure on a rigidified curve reduce, after multiplication by $r^{t.n}$, to the transported level structure of the reduction $A_0$ determines that structure uniquely. It is used in the existence-and-uniqueness statement for level transport and in the identification of the level-$n$ family over the Drinfeld moduli functor.
